# The platform administrators, who receive the fix of every platform fault. A tenant sees a
# platform fault as its effect and the statement that work resumes on its own, and the fix steps
# go to the destination named here. Copy this file to config/platform/platform-escalation.yaml in
# a checkout, or ship it in the platform config that PLATFORM_ENV_CONFIG_DIR holds in a
# deployment.
#
# administrators names an admin Slack channel, an admin email list, or both. With only email set
# the administrators receive email only, with only slack set they receive Slack only, and with
# both set they receive both. inv config-check, every activity worker at start, and the Slack
# posting service at start refuse a file that sets neither, and a missing file.
#
# slack.channel is the ID of the admin channel, which starts with C. The shared Slack app of
# slack-shared-app.yaml posts to it through the Slack posting service, so the channel requires
# that file and the shared bot's membership in the channel. slack.allowlist names who may press the
# buttons of an administrator alert under the repair kind, in the forms of a tenant's
# escalation.yaml. The kind is channel, which admits anyone in the admin channel and the shared
# app's organization, or a list of user_id and group_id entries. It defaults to channel. The
# inbound webhook reads the members of each group_id every 5 minutes, and a group whose members
# were not read in the last 10 minutes admits nobody.
#
# email lists the addresses of the administrators, each with a domain that holds a dot, such as
# admins@platform.test. The activity workers and the deployment-validate Job send to them over
# their SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, and ALERT_EMAIL_FROM settings.

config_version: 1

administrators:
  slack:
    channel: C0000000000
    allowlist:
      repair: channel
      # repair:
      #   - user_id: U0000000000
      #   - group_id: S0000000000
  email:
    - platform-admins@example.com
